Description: pRealm: bound REALMCAST_REQ subscriptions and reclaim expired pipeways
 Everything in a REALMCAST_REQ is chosen by whoever published it, and
 PipeWay::valid() only tests that the client is non-empty, the duration is
 non-zero, and there is either a channel or one variable:
   * the duration has no upper bound, so duration=1000000000 keeps a
     pipeway alive for about 31 years
   * the variable list is a ':' separated list with no length limit, and
     every variable is rendered on every interval
   * the client name is a map key, so one publisher can create as many
     distinct pipeways as it likes
 buildRealmCast() and buildWatchCast() serialise every live pipeway and
 publish it on every interval. 500 requests with a 31 year duration take
 pRealm from no measurable CPU to 1043 ticks and from 4.7 MB to 15 MB,
 .
 From upstream pull request #132, which is not merged yet: its four commits as one patch.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/moos-ivp/moos-ivp/pull/132
Forwarded: not-needed
Bug: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-85447
Last-Update: 2026-11-13
---
diff --git a/ivp/src/pRealm/PipeWay.cpp b/ivp/src/pRealm/PipeWay.cpp
index 48abdc1d..49058a93 100644
--- a/ivp/src/pRealm/PipeWay.cpp
+++ b/ivp/src/pRealm/PipeWay.cpp
@@ -115,6 +115,16 @@ bool PipeWay::valid() const
   if(m_client == "")
     return(false);
 
+  // The duration decides how long this pipeway keeps being serialised and
+  // published on every interval, and it comes from the request.
+  if((m_duration < 0) || (m_duration > MAX_PIPEWAY_DURATION))
+    return(false);
+
+  // The variable list is a ':' separated list in the request with no length
+  // of its own, and every variable is rendered every interval.
+  if(m_vars.size() > MAX_PIPEWAY_VARS)
+    return(false);
+
   return(true);
 }
 
